{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531","title":"Ethobot Setup and Debrief","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting ethobot setup and debrief to Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"03","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/08/02"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_ai_boundary"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Ethobot Setup and Debrief\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Ethobot Setup And Debrief, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-03-ethobot-setup-and-debrief.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Ethobot Setup and Debrief: Ethobot Setup And Debrief, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn ethics dialogue tool like Ethobot is only as valuable as the debrief that follows it. Students will argue, change their minds, or dig in, and the screen captures the whole transcript, but a transcript is not yet learning evidence. The teaching skill in this section is the debrief: reading a transcript closely enough to locate the exact turn where a student's ethical reasoning moved, and to name the prompt that caused the movement. A debrief that says \"good discussion\" preserves none of that. A debrief that pins the shift to a turn lets the teacher reuse the move that worked.\n\nThis matters because moral reasoning develops through specific provocations, not through general engagement. If you cannot say what reasoning a student held before a turn and after it, you cannot tell whether the activity changed anyone's thinking or merely entertained them. Locating the pivot is what turns a fun activity into documented intellectual growth.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA high-school class debates with Ethobot about whether the school should use facial recognition for attendance. Afterward the teacher writes: \"Great energy, students loved arguing with the bot.\" That note records enthusiasm but cannot answer the question that matters: did anyone's reasoning actually change, and what changed it? Buried in the transcript, one student moved from \"if it stops people sneaking in, just use it\" to \"facial recognition is too risky for the people it gets wrong.\" The whole point of the activity lives in that movement, and the celebratory note loses it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nDebriefing an ethics dialogue is the practice of marking reasoning movement, not measuring participation. A reasoning shift has three locatable parts in a transcript:\n\n- **The starting frame.** Quote the turn where the student states their initial reasoning and name the value it rests on. An efficiency-only frame (\"just use it if it works\") is different from a rights frame or an error-cost frame.\n- **The pivot.** Identify the specific turn, usually a question, where the frame breaks. The most productive pivots ask who bears the cost of being wrong, who is excluded, or what the student would say if they were the affected party.\n- **The revised frame.** Quote the turn where the student reasons differently and name the new value that has entered, such as fairness, disparate error cost, or rights.\n\nThe pedagogical payoff is that once you can name the pivot, you can reuse it. A question like \"who is most likely to be wrongly flagged?\" is portable: it can be turned on any classroom tool. Critical edtech studies supplies the disparate-impact lens that makes such pivots possible, since it directs attention to who is misread by a system. The bounded role of the tool also belongs in the debrief: Ethobot can provoke reasoning, but it should not be the authority that declares a student's position right or wrong; that judgment stays with the teacher and the class.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA weak debrief reports the climate of the room. A strong debrief locates the movement. Compare the two.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: an Ethobot debrief that marks where reasoning changed\n\nScenario: a high-school class ran an Ethobot dilemma about whether a school should use facial recognition for attendance. The debrief annotation must mark the exact transcript turn where a student's ethical reasoning shifted, not just say the discussion went well.\n\nBefore (debrief that reports tone, not movement):\n\n> Debrief note: Good engagement. Students enjoyed arguing with Ethobot about facial recognition.\n\nWalk the transcript and annotate the reasoning, turn by turn:\n\n1. Turn 4, starting position. Student: \"If it stops people sneaking in, just use it.\" This is a pure efficiency frame with no rights consideration.\n2. Turn 7, the pivot. Ethobot asks who is most likely to be misidentified. Student: \"Wait, if it works worse on darker skin then some kids get marked absent when they were there.\" This is the turn where a fairness and false-positive concern enters.\n3. Turn 9, revised position. Student: \"Maybe attendance by ID card is fine and facial recognition is too risky for the people it gets wrong.\" The reasoning moved from efficiency-only to a rights-and-error-cost frame.\n\nAfter (annotated Ethobot debrief):\n\n> Debrief annotation (08.03): Reasoning shift located at turn 7. Before turn 7 the student framed facial recognition as pure efficiency (turn 4). The pivot was the disparate false-positive prompt from Ethobot. After turn 7 the student weighed who bears the error cost and chose the lower-risk option (turn 9). Teaching move next: ask the class to generalize the turn-7 question (\"who is most likely to be wrongly flagged?\") to one other classroom tool.\n\nThe after annotation is stronger because it pins the ethical change to a specific transcript turn and the prompt that caused it, so another teacher can see what reasoning move actually did the work.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Debriefing the facial-recognition transcript\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The Ethobot dialogue on facial-recognition attendance just ended. The room was loud and engaged. How do you begin the debrief?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Note that the class had great energy and loved arguing with the bot.\",\"next\":\"warmth\",\"feedback\":\"Energy and reasoning change are different variables. A loud room can produce zero movement. This records climate, not learning.\"},{\"label\":\"Read the transcript for each student’s starting reasoning frame and the value it rests on.\",\"next\":\"frame\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Locating starting frames is the first step to seeing whether anyone actually moved.\"}]},\"warmth\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague asks: did anyone change their mind, and what changed it? Your note only says the energy was great. What can you tell them?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing specific; the note recorded enthusiasm.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"The one reusable thing the activity produced (the pivot) is now lost.\"},{\"label\":\"Go back to the transcript and locate the turn where a frame broke.\",\"next\":\"frame\",\"feedback\":\"Right move, but the debrief should have captured this the first time.\"}]},\"frame\":{\"prompt\":\"At turn 4 a student says: ’If it stops people sneaking in, just use it.’ What kind of frame is this?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A rights frame weighing who is harmed.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Not yet. There is no rights consideration here, only efficiency.\"},{\"label\":\"A pure efficiency frame with no consideration of error cost or rights.\",\"next\":\"pivot\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Naming the starting value lets you detect a later shift.\"}]},\"pivot\":{\"prompt\":\"At turn 7 Ethobot asks who is most likely to be misidentified, and the student reconsiders. What do you record?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"That the discussion got interesting around the middle.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Too vague to reuse. Pin the pivot to the turn and the prompt that caused it.\"},{\"label\":\"The pivot at turn 7: the disparate false-positive prompt; quote the before (turn 4) and after (turn 9) frames.\",\"next\":\"transfer\",\"feedback\":\"This is the located pivot, the evidence that reasoning actually moved.\"}]},\"transfer\":{\"prompt\":\"You have the pivot. Ethobot then declares the student is now ’correct.’ What do you do with that verdict, and what do you give the class next?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Record Ethobot’s verdict as the official answer and end the debrief.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"The tool provokes; it does not adjudicate whose ethics are right. That judgment stays with the teacher and class.\"},{\"label\":\"Set the tool verdict aside and ask the class to generalize the turn-7 question to another classroom tool.\",\"next\":\"end_strong\",\"feedback\":\"This keeps judgment with the class and turns the pivot into a portable, reusable question.\"}]},\"end_weak\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the debrief reports tone or defers to the tool. The reasoning shift, the one reusable product, is not located.\",\"feedback\":\"A debrief graded on warmth or on the tool verdict preserves nothing another teacher can adopt.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_strong\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the debrief quotes the starting frame, pins the pivot to turn 7 and its prompt, quotes the revised frame, and names a transferable question.\",\"feedback\":\"This is documented reasoning change a colleague can replay and reuse.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why locating the pivot turn (the specific prompt that moved a student's reasoning) is more valuable to your future teaching than recording that the discussion went well.\" answer=\"The pivot is portable. Once you can name the exact question that moved a student from an efficiency frame to a rights-and-error-cost frame, such as 'who is most likely to be wrongly flagged?', you can reuse that question on any other classroom tool or dilemma. Recording that the discussion went well preserves only the climate of one session; it gives you nothing to deploy again, because you never identified what reasoning move actually did the work.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen debriefing an ethics transcript, locate the reasoning frame first, mark the pivot turn second, and name the transferable question third. Before you treat a debrief as finished, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Frame check:** What value did the student's reasoning rest on before and after the shift?\n2. **Pivot check:** Which specific turn caused the movement, and what made it work?\n3. **Transfer check:** What portable question can the class now apply to another tool or dilemma?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\n<inline-check question=\"A debrief reads: 'Several students ended up agreeing that facial recognition is risky.' What is missing for this to count as documented reasoning change?\" explanation=\"It summarizes the conclusion but skips the pivot: the specific turn and prompt that moved the reasoning. Without the pivot, the one reusable product of the activity is discarded.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; reporting the shared conclusion is sufficient.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The pivot turn and the prompt that caused the shift, plus the before frame.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A count of how many students participated.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Ethobot final verdict on who was correct.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe most common error is grading the debrief on warmth: lively discussion is read as successful learning. Energy and reasoning change are different variables, and a class can have abundant energy with no movement at all. A second error is summarizing the conclusion and skipping the pivot, which discards the one reusable thing the activity produced. A third is letting the tool's verdict stand in for the teacher's judgment; Ethobot provokes, but it does not adjudicate whose ethics are correct.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine debriefing a transcript on whether students should be allowed to use AI to draft college essays. Read for the starting frames: some students will argue from convenience, others from fairness to peers who do not have the tool. Find the turn where a question reframes the issue, perhaps \"what is the essay supposed to show about you?\" Mark it. Then state what the class can carry forward, such as the habit of asking what an assessment is actually meant to measure before deciding whether a tool undermines it.\n\nThe artifact is the annotated transcript: starting frame quoted, pivot turn identified with the prompt that caused it, revised frame quoted, and the transferable question named for the next discussion.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a debrief by checking whether you could replay the moment of change from the annotation alone. If the note names a turn number, quotes the before and after, and identifies the provoking question, the reasoning is inspectable. Keep a two-column note while reviewing: the student's frame on the left, the turn and prompt that moved it on the right. A strong debrief lets a colleague adopt your most effective question without having watched the class.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nAn Ethobot debrief meets the standard when a mentor teacher can point to the exact turn where reasoning changed and the prompt that did the work. Reporting tone is not enough; the located pivot is the evidence. If the shift is asserted but not pinned to a turn, annotate the gap and ask BigAL, the course's revision-coaching assistant (a different tool from Ethobot, the ethics-dialogue provocateur whose transcript you are debriefing), to compare your debrief against an exemplar that locates a pivot rather than to summarize the discussion. A useful closing line reads: \"I located the reasoning shift at a specific turn and named the question that caused it, and I did not let Ethobot's framing stand in for the class's own judgment.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the debrief to a younger setting, a 5th-grade dialogue about whether a class points app should rank students publicly. The pivot questions change in vocabulary but not in kind: \"how would it feel to be last on the screen every day?\" is the age-appropriate version of \"who bears the cost?\" Annotating where a 5th grader's reasoning moves, rather than reusing the high-school annotation, shows that you can run the debrief practice across developmental levels.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-03-ethobot-setup-and-debrief-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Ethobot transcript annotation showing where ethical reasoning changed for CAT 531 08.03. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your annotation quotes the starting frame, pins the pivot to a turn, quotes the revised frame, and names the question the class can transfer. That is what separates documented reasoning change from a record of enthusiasm.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","stem":"An Ethobot debrief note reads: \"Good engagement. Students enjoyed arguing about facial recognition.\" What does this debrief fail to capture?","options":["How many students spoke during the activity.","Where in the transcript a student's ethical reasoning actually changed.","Whether the teacher liked the activity.","How long the activity lasted."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A debrief annotation should mark where reasoning moved, not just that students were engaged.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_engagement_not_reasoning","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-01","cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"At turn 4 a student says, \"If it stops people sneaking in, just use facial recognition.\" Which annotation best names the limit of this reasoning?","options":["The student is wrong and should be corrected.","This is an efficiency-only frame; it weighs whether the tool works but not who bears the error cost when it misidentifies a student.","The student is engaged, so the reasoning is strong.","The comment is too short to annotate."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Judging an ethical dilemma only on effectiveness ignores rights and disparate error costs, which is the reasoning gap the debrief should mark.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_efficiency_only_frame","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"What is the defensible way to treat Ethobot's questions during the dilemma?","options":["Whatever position Ethobot argues is the correct ethical answer.","Ethobot raises questions to provoke student judgment; students still weigh its prompts against the rights and costs in the case.","Students should not respond to Ethobot at all.","Ethobot should be used only to grade the discussion."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Ethobot prompts reasoning but does not settle the ethics. Students judge its prompts rather than adopt them.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_treating_output_as_verdict","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_setup_and_debrief_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which debrief annotation gives the strongest evidence that ethical reasoning changed?","options":["Reasoning improved overall during the lesson.","Shift located at turn 7: the disparate false-positive prompt moved the student from an efficiency-only frame (turn 4) to weighing who bears the error cost (turn 9).","Students grew a lot this unit.","The discussion went better than last time."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest annotation pins the change to a specific turn and the question that caused it, so another teacher can see what reasoning move did the work.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_no_pivot_located","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ethobot_setup_and_debrief","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-03-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_engagement_not_reasoning","pattern":"reporting_engagement_instead_of_reasoning_change","description":"Writing an Ethobot debrief that reports that students enjoyed the activity rather than marking where ethical reasoning actually moved","trigger":"debrief note describes mood or participation but no shift in argument","feedback":"Mark where the reasoning changed, not whether students had fun. Find the turn that moved the argument.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_efficiency_only_frame","pattern":"judging_a_tool_on_efficiency_only","description":"Evaluating an ethical dilemma (such as facial recognition for attendance) only on whether it works, ignoring who bears the error cost or rights at stake","trigger":"student or annotation frames the decision purely as effectiveness","feedback":"Add the rights-and-error frame. Ask who is most likely to be wrongly affected, not only whether the tool works.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_no_pivot_located","pattern":"annotation_with_no_located_pivot","description":"Annotating that reasoning improved without pinning the change to a specific transcript turn or the prompt that caused it","trigger":"debrief claims growth but cites no turn number or triggering question","feedback":"Locate the pivot. Name the turn where the reasoning changed and the question that caused it.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_3_ethobot_treating_output_as_verdict","pattern":"treating_ethobot_output_as_the_ethical_verdict","description":"Accepting Ethobot's framing as the correct ethical answer instead of treating it as a prompt for student judgment","trigger":"student adopts the bot's position without weighing it","feedback":"Ethobot raises questions, it does not settle them. Judge its prompts against the rights and costs in the case.","rail_action":"practice"}